Open DevTools with F12 or Ctrl+Shift+I (Cmd+Option+I on Mac).

1. Console: find errors first

Red errors show JavaScript exceptions, failed resource loads, and CORS or CSP violations, each with a file and line link.
Turn on Preserve log so messages survive page reloads and redirects.
Filter by level (Errors, Warnings) or search text to cut noise.

2. Network: the main tool for most web app issues

Reload with the tab open and Disable cache checked, to rule out stale assets.
Sort or filter by Fetch/XHR to see API calls.
Click a request to check:
Status code: 4xx means client or auth problems (401/403 auth, 404 wrong path, 422/400 bad payload), 5xx means server errors.
Headers: request URL and method, Authorization, Content-Type, Origin, CORS response headers, cache headers.
Payload: what the client actually sent.
Response/Preview: the error body, which often names the real cause.
Timing: a long “waiting for server response” points to the backend, and a long “content download” points to payload size.
Right-click a request and choose Copy > Copy as cURL to replay it in a terminal or hand it to engineering. Remove tokens and cookies before sharing.
Throttling (for example “Slow 4G” or “Offline”) reproduces slow-network and failure behavior.

3. Sources: debug JavaScript

Open a file, click a line number to set a breakpoint, and trigger the action.
Step with F10 (over), F11 (into), and Shift+F11 (out), and watch Scope and Watch values.
Use Pause on exceptions (the pause icon) to stop at the failing line.
Event listener breakpoints and XHR/fetch breakpoints help when you don’t know where the code lives.

4. Elements: HTML and CSS problems

Inspect an element to see its computed styles, box model, and which rule wins.
Toggle or edit styles live, and check for display: none, overflow, z-index, or a parent clipping content.
Use Break on > subtree/attribute modifications to catch what script changes an element.

5. Application: storage and auth state

Cookies, Local/Session Storage, IndexedDB: check whether the token or session value exists, and whether cookie flags (Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite, expiry) explain a login problem.
Service Workers and Cache Storage: use Unregister or Clear site data when a page keeps showing old code.

6. Performance and Lighthouse

Performance records a page load or interaction to find long tasks and layout thrash.
Lighthouse runs quick audits for speed, accessibility, and best practices.

A simple troubleshooting order

Reproduce the problem with the Console and Network tabs open.
Check the Console for errors.
Find the failing or slow request in Network and read its status, request, and response.
Compare what the client sent with what the API expects.
If it’s a front-end logic issue, set a breakpoint in Sources.
Rule out cache and storage by hard-reloading or using an Incognito window.

Security notes

HAR exports, cURL copies, and screenshots can contain session tokens, cookies, and personal data. Redact them before sharing, and never put them in a public repo.
Only test sites and apps you have permission to examine.
